watering her crops hilary pavatt has  repurposed her garden to grow vegetables  to help eventually feed herself and her  family during the coronavirus crisis  she's one of millions of Britons now  sowing seeds to try and become more  self-sufficient after realizing that  food supply chains are fragile  we've started planting potatoes we've  got vegetables in cut again in salads  stuff for the winter so hopefully in  about I'd say six weeks we'll start to  be self self-sufficient so I think and  obviously there's going to be the  problem with how produce is going to end  up in the supermarket's because you know  they can't get the migrant workers to  come in  there's lockdown so you know in the end  there's going to end up being shortages  I think you know in weeks to come so  hopefully I hope I'll be all right  others without gardens are using what  space they have to germinate tomatoes  cucumbers and herbs Britain's wartime  generation were told to dig for victory  and responded with enthusiasm  having Britain's reliance on food  imports by the early 1940s  now households with time on their hands  are returning to the garden the Royal  Horticultural Society has seen a surge  in visits to its website seeking advice  on how to grow your own vegetables fruit  and edible plants while seed  manufacturers are reporting a massive  rise in sales in recent weeks in  supermarkets most panic buying has eased  but egg shelves are often still bare  considered a staple of the pandemic  country live poultry breeders across the  UK have seen their farms stripped of  birds as families unable to find eggs in  the shops turned to chicken keeping  Sussex poultry farmer Liz and Roxie has  now decided to stop selling chickens  anxious that some might regret their  impulse buys when the lockdown is lifted  and I've never had so many people call  text Nemo like I said it takes me a good  month to sell the stock that I get in  and within a week I had sold all my some  of my leftover February birds and my mom  watch Birds so it was that huge panic I  think for people to suddenly go oh my  god I can't get my eggs the UK currently  imports 85% of its vegetables from the  European Union but there are concerns  lengthy quarantines could disrupt food  supply chains turning to the soil rather  than the supermarket for fresh produce  could ease that burden charlie Angela al  Jazeera Hampshire
 

